<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-objects-classes" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Objects and Classes</title>

  <section xml:id="sec-6-1-overview">
  <title>Chapter Overview</title>
    <p>
      In Chapter 5, you learned how to manage the complexity of larger programs 
      through functional decomposition: breaking a problem into independent subtasks 
      that can be designed, tested, and debugged individually. You learned to write 
      static helper methods that fulfill responsibilities for <c>main</c>, taking in 
      data through parameters and returning a result. You learned to document 
      preconditions and postconditions and design unit tests for your methods. You also 
      saw how the Java Virtual Machine allocates and reclaims memory on the call stack 
      as methods execute, and how scope rules isolate local variables within their 
      respective methods.
    </p>

    <p>
      In this chapter, we take the next major step in software design. Rather than keeping 
      data in loose variables inside <c>main</c> and passing them back and forth to 
      static methods, we will combine data and the methods that operate on that data into 
      a single unit called an <em>object</em>. By writing object definitions, called 
      <em>classes</em>, you will create your own custom data types tailored to the 
      real-world concepts in your programs. You already know how to declare variables and 
      write methods<mdash/>now we will unite those skills to build self-contained, reusable 
      software components.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Roadmap for Chapter 6</strong>
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-6-2-oop-intro" text="title"/>: Exploring programming 
          paradigms, contrasting procedural decomposition with object-oriented design, 
          and understanding how encapsulation and information hiding protect data 
          integrity.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-6-3-structure" text="title"/>: Exploring how object-oriented 
          programs are organized across multiple files in an IDE, examining a minimal 
          two-file example, and tracing how objects are constructed on the heap, how 
          reference variables track them on the stack, and how accessor and mutator 
          methods query and update their state.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-6-4-defining-class" text="title"/>: 
		  Writing a class blueprint 
          by declaring <c>private</c> instance variables (fields) to hold state, 
          implementing <c>public</c> constructors, accessors (getters), and mutators 
          (setters) to define behavior, and visualizing classes with UML class and 
          object diagrams.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-6-5-static-instance" text="title"/>: Distinguishing between 
          instance members tied to individual heap objects and <c>static</c> members 
          shared across an entire class blueprint, declaring named constants 
          (<c>public static final</c>), maintaining shared counters, and understanding 
          when to use static utility methods versus instance methods.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-6-6-overloading" text="title"/>: Overloading constructors and 
          instance methods to provide flexible object initialization, writing default and 
          argument constructors, using the <c>this</c> reference to resolve local 
          variable shadowing, and chaining constructors with <c>this(...)</c> to 
          eliminate redundant initialization logic.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-6-7-memory-model" text="title"/>: Tracing how reference 
          variables on the call stack point to objects on the heap, understanding reference 
          aliasing (<c>=</c>), contrasting shallow copying with deep copying, passing 
          object references as method arguments, defending against <c>null</c> references, 
          and visualizing how Java's garbage collector reclaims unreachable memory.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-6-8-tostring-equals" text="title"/>: Tapping into Java's 
          universal <c>Object</c> hierarchy to replace default memory-address output with 
          custom <c>toString()</c> formatting and value-based <c>equals()</c> comparisons.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-6-9-javadoc" text="title"/>: Authoring professional Javadoc 
          documentation that adheres to information hiding, specifying <c>@param</c> and 
          <c>@return</c> tags without leaking implementation details, and formally 
          documenting class invariants in class-level header comments.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-6-10-unit-testing" text="title"/>: Testing stateful objects 
          using the industry-standard Arrange<ndash/>Act<ndash/>Assert (AAA) pattern, 
          validating state transitions, and enforcing method preconditions with Java 
          assertions.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <xref ref="sec-6-11-case-study" text="title"/>: Applying object-oriented design 
          to a multi-class system inspired by a shared-use community kitchen, using 
          Class<ndash/>Responsibility<ndash/>Collaborator (CRC) cards to decompose 
          responsibilities, modeling unidirectional composition (<q>has-a</q> 
          relationships) between <c>RecipeBatch</c> and <c>Oven</c>, tracing lifecycle 
          changes with a UML state-transition diagram, and walking through full 
          implementation and verification.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Once you master the concepts in this chapter, you will no longer be limited to 
      Java's built-in types. You will be able to design, document, test, and connect your 
      own custom data types to model any problem domain. Let's get started.
    </p>
  </section>

